  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that the randomly selected player is American, and let $S$ be the event that a player speaks Spanish. Then, we require $P(A|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(A|S)=\\frac{P(S|A)P(A)}{P(S)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- $P(S|A)=0.15,$ since $15%$ of all American players on the team speak spanish.\n\n- $P(A)=0.75,$ since $0.75%$ of the players on the team are American citizens.\n\n- $P(S)=0.25,$ since  $25%$ of all players on the team speak Spanish.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|S) & =\\frac{P(S|A)P(A)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.15)(0.75)}{0.25} \\\\  & =0.45.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
